Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.90,0:00:03.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Shine white light on a surface with fine,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.15,0:00:07.34,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}regular grooves, like a CD, and it fans out into rainbows.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:07.41,0:00:11.09,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Stack thin, clear layers, like those in a morpho butterfly's wing,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.13,0:00:13.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and they reflect one vivid colour.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.60,0:00:15.91,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}No pigment anywhere. So how can mere
Dialogue: 0,0:00:15.95,0:00:18.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}structure pick a direction, and a colour?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.24,0:00:20.97,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}It all comes down to one idea: symmetry.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.79,0:00:24.00,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Start with the simplest structure:
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.04,0:00:26.99,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}a flat boundary, with glass below and air above.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:27.06,0:00:32.07,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Remember from episode one: a plane wave's tilt is set by kx,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:32.11,0:00:36.63,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}its frequency along x. Then sin θ = kx/k.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:36.70,0:00:40.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}In glass the wavelength shrinks, so k grows to n·k0,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:40.22,0:00:45.63,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}where n is the refractive index. Each medium gets its own circle.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.70,0:00:47.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The field can't jump at the boundary,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:47.32,0:00:49.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so the crests on both sides must meet.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.76,0:00:54.34,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Both waves share one kx: it's conserved, like momentum.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:54.41,0:00:57.65,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}In k-space, a shared kx is one vertical line.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:57.69,0:00:59.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Where it crosses each circle,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:59.32,0:01:04.13,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}you read off each wave's direction. That's Snell's law, in one picture.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:04.20,0:01:07.93,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now tilt the incoming wave further. The line slides outward,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.97,0:01:10.75,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and soon it misses the air circle completely.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:11.52,0:01:15.19,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}No wave in air has that kx, so all the light reflects,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:15.23,0:01:19.79,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}leaving only an evanescent tail. That's total internal reflection.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.61,0:01:24.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now give the boundary a pattern that repeats every Λ,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.50,0:01:27.20,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}the period. That's a grating.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:27.27,0:01:31.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Anything that repeats like this is a Fourier series:
Dialogue: 0,0:01:31.55,0:01:36.97,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}a sum of waves e^(imKx), where K = 2π/Λ.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:37.04,0:01:40.65,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Passing through the grating multiplies the light by these waves,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.69,0:01:43.94,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and each one shifts kx by mK.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.01,0:01:48.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So kx is now conserved only up to multiples of K.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:48.47,0:01:52.61,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}In k-space: copies of the line, spaced by K.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.68,0:01:54.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Each copy that crosses the circle is a
Dialogue: 0,0:01:54.84,0:01:57.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}beam that can leave: a diffraction order.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.46,0:02:01.49,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Colour sets the circle. Red light has a smaller circle than blue,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:01.53,0:02:05.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so the same K bends red the most: a rainbow.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:05.15,0:02:07.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now shrink the period. K grows,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:07.46,0:02:10.49,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and the orders leave the circle, one by one.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:11.36,0:02:14.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}At normal incidence into air, a period below the air
Dialogue: 0,0:02:14.59,0:02:17.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}wavelength leaves only the transmitted zero order.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:17.60,0:02:19.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Internal resonances can remain,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.84,0:02:24.30,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so treating the grating as a uniform film needs a separate check.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:25.12,0:02:28.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now let the structure repeat along the direction light travels:
Dialogue: 0,0:02:28.56,0:02:31.45,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}a stack of layers, repeating every period.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:31.52,0:02:34.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Shift it by one period and nothing changes.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:34.29,0:02:36.83,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So the waves inside can only gain a
Dialogue: 0,0:02:36.87,0:02:39.35,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}fixed phase from one period to the next.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:39.42,0:02:45.20,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}These are Bloch waves: e^(iκz) times a function that repeats.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:45.24,0:02:48.15,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A full turn of phase per period is invisible,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:48.19,0:02:50.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so κ only matters up to multiples of K.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:51.02,0:02:53.07,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Plot frequency, ω, against κ.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:53.11,0:02:56.41,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}With no contrast, light follows a straight line,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.45,0:03:00.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and its copies, shifted by K, are the same waves.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:00.63,0:03:04.22,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The copies cross at the zone edge, where λ = 2nΛ.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:04.26,0:03:09.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}That's the Bragg condition: reflections from every layer add up in step.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:09.75,0:03:13.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now turn up the index contrast, Δn. Watch the crossing:
Dialogue: 0,0:03:13.80,0:03:17.49,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}the forward and backward waves couple, and the bands pull apart.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:18.36,0:03:21.03,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A gap opens: a band of frequencies where
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.07,0:03:23.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}no wave can travel through the stack.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:23.15,0:03:26.13,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Light there dies off within a few layers, so it all reflects.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:26.17,0:03:30.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Ten pairs make a nearly perfect mirror for one band of colours.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:31.60,0:03:34.61,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}How do you compute a real grating, with thick ridges?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:34.65,0:03:39.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Rigorous coupled-wave analysis, RCWA, puts all of this together.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:40.02,0:03:42.89,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Thanks to the period, the field in any layer is just
Dialogue: 0,0:03:42.93,0:03:45.99,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}a list of amplitudes, one per diffraction order.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:46.06,0:03:50.45,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}In a uniform layer, each order travels alone, picking up its own phase,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:50.49,0:03:55.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}e^(ikz z). The matrix is diagonal, as in episode one.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:55.53,0:03:59.09,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}In a grating layer, the Fourier coefficients of ε couple
Dialogue: 0,0:03:59.13,0:04:02.13,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}each order to the others, and the matrix fills in.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:02.20,0:04:06.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Diagonalize it: the eigenvectors are the layer's Bloch modes,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:06.29,0:04:08.49,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and the eigenvalues give their kz.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:08.56,0:04:12.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Advance each mode's phase, match the fields at every interface,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:12.22,0:04:14.60,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and chain the layers with scattering matrices.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.64,0:04:18.91,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}They never contain growing exponentials, so they stay stable.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:19.73,0:04:24.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}On a computer, RCWA takes four lines: expand ε,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:24.22,0:04:27.69,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}build the matrix, diagonalize, chain the layers.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:27.76,0:04:30.89,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The pitfall is truncation: keep enough orders,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:30.93,0:04:33.35,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and check that the answer converges.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:33.39,0:04:36.93,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}For the other polarization, use Li's factorization rules.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:37.00,0:04:40.35,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The payoff: sweep the ridge width of a sub-wavelength grating,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:40.39,0:04:43.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and record the phase of the light passing straight through.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:44.15,0:04:45.99,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}That's a metasurface's unit-cell library.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:46.03,0:04:48.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Pick the width for each phase you need,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:48.50,0:04:51.44,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and you have a flat lens, cell by cell.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:52.26,0:04:57.37,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Symmetry fixes momentum. Flat interfaces conserve kx.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:57.41,0:05:02.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Periodic ones conserve it only up to multiples of K = 2π/Λ,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:02.22,0:05:04.90,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and where copies meet, gaps open.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:04.97,0:05:08.36,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Where it breaks: RCWA needs true periodicity,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:08.40,0:05:12.75,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and a metasurface lens only borrows it locally, cell by cell.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:12.82,0:05:15.49,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Total internal reflection traps light in a slab,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:15.53,0:05:17.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and Bragg mirrors trap it between them.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:17.57,0:05:20.32,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So how do we guide light, and store it?
